Quoth Karl Garrison <kgarrison@pobox.com> on this day of Sat, 04 Oct
2003 07:54:44 -0500:

>What do you think of this idea: instead of a flat amount of extra damage,
>the extra damage is 1d(damage dealt * 2).  In other words, if the elf would
>normally have taken 2 points of damage from an iron weapon, the damage
>ends-up being 2+1d4.  This scales well with level, so that the elf won't
>die in the first dart trap he stumbles into, and also makes sense
>logically.  Perhaps the extra damage should only be from piercing and
>slashing weapons.

If I understand you correctly, this would mean that an ordinary
dagger, wielded by (say) a goblin, would do from 2 to 12 hp damage,
instead of the 1 to 4 hp currently. A dart would do from 2 to 9.

Depending on role (restricted to Wiz, Priest, or Ranger, iirc), an elf
would start with 11, 13, or 14 hp. The first dart trap could
potentially take 3/4 of his starting hp.

Further down in the dungeon, a band of orcs wielding scimitars would
do 2-24 hp per whack, each.

Seems to me it's still a bit too much over the top. The damage bonus
is far higher (in most cases) than that of blessed weapons vs. undead
or demon (1d4) and depending on weapon could rival that of silver vs.
silver-hating monsters (1d20).
